{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"f8871aad15008c50","name":"card_event_your_courage","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/Homefront/events/card_event_your_courage","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"YOUR COURAGE","text":"Reduce the cost of cards with Bond in hand by 1. Then your Bond units everywhere get +1+1.","flavorText":"In 1939, as Britain prepared for war, the Ministry of Information launched a poster campaign to bolster civilian morale. The first two designs were quickly printed: “Your courage, your cheerfulness, your resolution will bring us victory” and “Freedom is in peril. Defend it with all your might.” The goal was to maintain public confidence during expected air raids and hardships. These posters appeared across shop windows, public transport, and official buildings in the early months of the war. The bold red backgrounds and serif white lettering beneath a stylized crown projected authority and calm. “Your courage” aimed to show unity between the government and the people, placing the burden of perseverance not on soldiers alone but on every British citizen. Unlike “Keep calm and carry on,” which was held in reserve and never widely released, “Your courage” was among the most visible wartime slogans in Britain during 1939 and 1940. Despite its wide distribution, public response was mixed. Some saw it as inspiring, others as patronizing. But it remains a striking example of how wartime British propaganda used simple design and direct language to prepare the homefront for the trials ahead.","faction":"Britain","type":"order","rarity":"Unique","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_your_courage","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_your_courage_title","SourceString":"YOUR COURAGE","LocalizedString":"YOUR COURAGE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_your_courage_text","SourceString":"Reduce the cost of cards with Bond in hand by 1. Then your Bond units everywhere get +1+1.","LocalizedString":"Reduce the cost of cards with Bond in hand by 1. Then your Bond units everywhere get +1+1."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_your_courage_history","SourceString":"In 1939, as Britain prepared for war, the Ministry of Information launched a poster campaign to bolster civilian morale. The first two designs were quickly printed: “Your courage, your cheerfulness, your resolution will bring us victory” and “Freedom is in peril. Defend it with all your might.” The goal was to maintain public confidence during expected air raids and hardships. These posters appeared across shop windows, public transport, and official buildings in the early months of the war. The bold red backgrounds and serif white lettering beneath a stylized crown projected authority and calm. “Your courage” aimed to show unity between the government and the people, placing the burden of perseverance not on soldiers alone but on every British citizen. Unlike “Keep calm and carry on,” which was held in reserve and never widely released, “Your courage” was among the most visible wartime slogans in Britain during 1939 and 1940. Despite its wide distribution, public response was mixed. Some saw it as inspiring, others as patronizing. But it remains a striking example of how wartime British propaganda used simple design and direct language to prepare the homefront for the trials ahead.","LocalizedString":"In 1939, as Britain prepared for war, the Ministry of Information launched a poster campaign to bolster civilian morale. The first two designs were quickly printed: “Your courage, your cheerfulness, your resolution will bring us victory” and “Freedom is in peril. Defend it with all your might.” The goal was to maintain public confidence during expected air raids and hardships. These posters appeared across shop windows, public transport, and official buildings in the early months of the war. The bold red backgrounds and serif white lettering beneath a stylized crown projected authority and calm. “Your courage” aimed to show unity between the government and the people, placing the burden of perseverance not on soldiers alone but on every British citizen. Unlike “Keep calm and carry on,” which was held in reserve and never widely released, “Your courage” was among the most visible wartime slogans in Britain during 1939 and 1940. Despite its wide distribution, public response was mixed. Some saw it as inspiring, others as patronizing. But it remains a striking example of how wartime British propaganda used simple design and direct language to prepare the homefront for the trials ahead."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'For_the_Queen'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/For_the_Queen.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/YOUR_COURAGE.YOUR_COURAGE","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":325.0,"Y":512.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_your_courage_photo","SourceString":"A young boy with a helmet standing in front of the “Your Courage” poster. Ca. 1940.","LocalizedString":"A young boy with a helmet standing in front of the “Your Courage” poster. Ca. 1940."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"Notes":"Image needed to be customized","isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_your_courage","owner":"card_event_your_courage_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32","di